narrative:
## Borrows, Contracts, and Queues

A queue has two operations: enqueue (add) and dequeue (remove). Both mutate the queue, so both need `&mut` borrows. Both have preconditions that contracts enforce:

```xiom
type Queue = {
  items: Vec[Int];
  capacity: Int;
  invariant: items.len() <= capacity;
}

fn enqueue(q: &mut Queue, item: Int)
  requires: q.items.len() < q.capacity;
  ensures: q.items.len() == q@pre.items.len() + 1;
{
  q.items.push(item);
}

fn dequeue(q: &mut Queue) -> Option[Int]
  requires: q.items.len() > 0;
  ensures: q.items.len() == q@pre.items.len() - 1;
{
  let first = q.items[0];
  q.items.remove(0);
  return Some(first);
}
```

### How Borrows and Contracts Work Together

The borrow checker (`&mut`) guarantees no one else can read or write the queue during enqueue/dequeue. Contracts guarantee the queue is in a valid state for the operation. Together:

1. **Borrows prevent TOCTOU races** — no other code can change `q.items.len()` between the requires check and the body
2. **Contracts prevent LOGICAL errors** — you can't enqueue on a full queue, can't dequeue from an empty one

```xiom
fn is_full(q: &Queue) -> Bool {
  return q.items.len() == q.capacity;
}
```

`is_full` takes `&Queue` (immutable borrow) because it only reads. No contract needed — it's always safe to ask if a queue is full. The caller uses this to decide whether to enqueue or wait.

